 Evaluatable->False,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.891096538433184*^9, 3.89109654180459*^9}, 
   3.9096694551762733`*^9, {3.911046016610118*^9, 3.91104601883628*^9}, {
   3.990264821673855*^9, 3.99026483990554*^9}, {3.990264888163412*^9, 
   3.990264888991114*^9}, {3.990715900524481*^9, 
   3.990715928549366*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dc0e2cb7-3e62-e941-9030-\
d92d4e06297b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Image trimming:\nIf usetrim = \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]yes\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], enter the minimum and maximum coordinates into the \
parameter table to use for the ImageTrim function. To obtain the correct \
trimming dimensions, right-click one of the original images (within the \
original images display) and select Get Coordinates. The display supports \
drag zoom functionality allowing to first zoom into a region then right click \
and select Get Coordinates. Hover over the image, then find and enter the min \
{x,y} and max {x,y} coordinates for the region of interest (ROI) into the \
parameter table. Here, the ROI is the area of the image that does not contain \
SEM information (i.e., the area we want to analyze). Use the same technique \
to find the ROI coordinates of the scale bar and its magnitude.\n\nPixel \
size:\nScale bar ROI: To obtain the correct ROI dimensions, start by \
right-clicking one of the original images and selecting get coordinates. \
Hover over the image, then find and enter the min {x,y} and max {x,y} \
coordinates for the scale bar (ROI) into the parameter table. Here, the scale \
bar ROI is the area of the image that contains the scale bar (leaving a small \
amount of padding around it). Use drag zoom to make this step simpler. \n\
Bounding box: Input a gray value threshold (SBthresh, number between 0 and \
1), minimum component length (minLen, in pixels), and whether the scale bar \
image should be color negated (If the scale bar is black with a white \
background, input \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]yes\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], \
otherwise enter \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]no\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]). These \
parameters are used to edit the scale bar image and find a bounding box in \
scalebarmeas.  \nScale bar bounding box index: Each bounding box has an index \
associated with it. If the scale bar is a continuous line, it should have one \
identifying index (shown in scalebarhighlight). Enter this value into the SB \
highlight column in the parameter table. If the scale bar is split into \
multiple segments, each segment should have its corresponding identifying \
index. In this case, enter the indices for the first and last segments, \
formatted as {first index, last index}, in the parameter table.\nScale bar \
Magnitude: As before, use the select coordinates functionality to find the \
ROI for the scale bar magnitude and add these into the parameter table in \
Magnitude ROI. Enter the corresponding scale bar magnitude value and unit in \
the parameter table. Check that the entered magnitude information corresponds \
with the image information. Make sure to use the same units for all imported \
images. For example, if an image has a scale bar magnitude of 1 um but you \
want to conduct the width analysis in ",
 StyleBox["nm",
  IgnoreSpellCheck->True],
 " make sure to input 1000 in the magnitude value column. \n\nSegmented \
images:\nHigh-quality binary images where the foreground is white and the \
background is black are necessary for FACT analysis. For this, follow the \
steps to apply the trained U^2-Net to the trimmed gray images. The U^2-Net \
segmentation step can be skipped if the user added binary images (perhaps \
obtained by using a different method or from a different program) to the \
Segmented images folder.\n\nBinary modification:\nThe binary image can be \
modified before skeletonization to improve the quality of the width analysis. \
Here, the binary modification consists of a Gaussian filter (to smooth jagged \
fibril edges) and a component selection filter. The component selection \
filter allows selecting binary connected components (particles) with a pixel \
count and elongation parameter greater than a threshold value. In the \
parameter table, input a filter size if you wish to use a Gaussian filter on \
the binary images. Suggested values range from 0 to 5. Set to 0 to omit this \
filter. Input the minimum component pixel count (0 to a large number) and \
minimum elongation (0 to 1) to remove small and or round connected components \
(particles) in the binary images. Set these parameters to 0 to omit these \
filters.\n\nSkeleton segment refining:\nSkeleton segment refining consists of \
a trimming step followed by filtering spurious skeleton segments. \nSST: Trim \
individual skeleton segments by eliminating data points from the ends. \
Accepted values range from 0 to 100%. Recommended values from 2% to 50% .\n\
SSF: Input an angle value (0 to 60 deg) to remove skeleton segments that have \
a mean distance transform rate of change greater than this value. Input a \
number larger than 60 to omit this filter. \nTrimming type: enter 1, and the \
SST function will not trim from the skeleton segment side adjacent to an \
endpoint. Entering 2 trims from both ends. \nOrientation effect: enter \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]yes\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] if you wish to account for \
the skeleton segment orientation effect (raster effect) on the pixel counting \
statistics. This is recommended. Otherwise, enter \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]no\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].\nSS min count: input a minimum pixel count to \
filter small skeleton segments. Enter 0 to omit this filter.\nJoin ",
 StyleBox["jp",
  IgnoreSpellCheck->True],
 " dist: input the maximum radial distance (pixels) used to concatenate \
nearby junction points. Enter 0 to omit.\n\nOverlay scale bar:\nInput the \
parameters used to overlay a scale bar on filtbranchdispl. xFrac and yFrac \
are the location of the center of the scale bar as fractional coordinates, ",
 StyleBox["len",
  IgnoreSpellCheck->True],
 " is the total length of the scale bar interpreted as having the units \
defined in the pixel size determination section, color refers to the color of \
the scale bar and scale magnitude value, magShift controls the distance \
between the scale magnitude value and the scale bar, fontSize controls the \
size of the scale magnitude value, and unit refers to the new unit the user \
wishes to display (FACT adjusts the value of the magnitude based on this unit \
change).\n\nOverlay graphics:\nInput a point size for the skeleton segments \
to use in the skeleton overlay graphics. An appropriate range is between \
0.0010 and 0.0045.\n\nStatistical Analysis:\nMultby2: The distance transform \
results in a fibril radial dimension. Write \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]yes\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]  if you wish to multiply all the distance transform \
values by 2 to approximate a full-width measurement. Otherwise write \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]no\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. \nGroup names: To consider \
selected images as a group for statistical analysis, label corresponding \
images using the same group name. \nHistogram: For the stacked-width \
histograms, enter a bin size (based on the unit used in pixel size \
determination) and plot ranges. Here, x represents the width range and y the \
probability."
}], "CodeText",
 Editable->"False",
 Deletable->"False",
 Evaluatable->False,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.891342718104278*^9, 3.891342725635096*^9}, {
